WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:11.640
Security headers can be applied in several places: application code, web servers, reverse proxies, CDNs, load balancers, service meshes, and cloud platform configuration.

00:00:11.860 --> 00:00:14.900
The right location depends on ownership and consistency.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:19.360
Some headers are best controlled by the application because they vary by route or page.

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:21.500
Others can be standardized at the edge.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:26.360
Teams should know where each header is set and which layer wins when configurations overlap.

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:29.220
Environment-specific configuration matters.

00:00:29.440 --> 00:00:38.800
Development, staging, and production may use different domains, script sources, API endpoints, CDNs, identity providers, and partner integrations.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:44.140
A policy that is perfect in production may break staging if staging uses different hostnames.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.360
A staging policy that allows everything may hide production problems.

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:54.180
Mature teams define environment differences deliberately and avoid letting temporary exceptions become permanent.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.120
Testing should combine tools and real workflows.

00:00:57.340 --> 00:01:03.900
Header scanners can identify missing or weak headers, but they cannot always know whether a policy matches application behavior.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:10.840
Browser developer tools can show blocked resources, mixed content warnings, CSP reports, and frame restrictions.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:15.780
Automated checks in CI/CD can verify expected headers on key routes.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:23.280
Manual validation should cover authentication, embedded pages, uploads, downloads, and third-party integrations.

00:01:23.500 --> 00:01:26.060
Avoid cargo-cult copy-paste header sets.

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.400
A header list from a blog may be a useful starting point, but every directive should have a reason.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.060
Document exceptions, owners, and review dates.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:46.720
Revisit headers when architecture changes, when a new frontend framework is introduced, when a CDN changes, when partners are added, or when a page starts using browser capabilities it did not previously need.

00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:50.140
Headers are configuration, but they are also security design.
